                <pb facs="0277" n="157" sameAs="frus:frus1932v01/pg157"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A4/1098: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Chairman of the American Delegation
                            (Gibson) to the
                            Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1932-06-07T13:00:00+01:00">June 7, 1932—1
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received June 7—8:10 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>233. For the Secretary and the President. Informed in strict confidence by member
                    of the British delegation that they understand Sir
                        John Simon has come out whole-heartedly for Baldwin’s plan reported in our 169, May 13, 4
                    p.m., from London; that the Cabinet was to meet to consider the plan last night
                    and will meet again tomorrow, Wednesday morning, and that it is hoped that the
                    plan will be accepted as a whole for presentation at Geneva. Our informant was
                    not aware of the details of the plan merely knowing that it was of a general
                    character.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gibson</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
